{"name":"Velour Scar","hex":"#8e5164","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"velour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A knit fabric similar to velvet, but usually somewhat coarser."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/velour"},{"term":"scar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A permanent mark on the skin, sometimes caused by the healing of a wound.","A permanent negative effect on someone's mind, caused by a traumatic experience."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mark the skin permanently.","To form a scar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Velour","blocks":[{"text":"Velour, occasionally velours, is a plush, knitted fabric or textile similar to velvet or velveteen. It can be made from polyester, spandex, cotton, or a cotton-polyester blend. Velour is used in a wide variety of applications, including clothing and upholstery. Velour typically has a medium-length pile, shorter than velvet but longer than velveteen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/PinkBathrobe.JPG/330px-PinkBathrobe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velour"},{"title":"Scar","blocks":[{"text":"A scar is an area of fibrous tissue that replaces normal skin following an injury. Scars result from the biological process of wound repair in the skin, as well as in other organs and tissues of the body. Thus, scarring is a natural part of the healing process. With the exception of very minor lesions, every wound results in some degree of scarring. An exception is animals with complete regeneration, which regrow tissue without scar formation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Scar_%28xndr%29.jpg/330px-Scar_%28xndr%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scar"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thum